        "findings": [
          {
            "file": "mise.toml",
            "summary": "Without `--run`, the `eddy-review` task expands an empty array under `set -u`, so it fails before Eddy starts when the shell is bash 3.2 (macOS's `/bin/bash`).",
            "detail": "The script sets `lock=()` and fills it only when `--run` is passed. It then runs `\"${lock[@]}\" scripts/dev-bin.sh ...` under `set -euo pipefail`. Bash before 4.4 treats expanding an empty array as an unbound variable and aborts with `lock[@]: unbound variable`. That is the common case, a plain `mise run eddy-review`, on any host where `#!/usr/bin/env bash` finds the system bash 3.2, as on stock macOS. `scripts/exclusive.sh` has a fallback for hosts without `flock`, which suggests such hosts are expected. Not reproduced (no tool to run it here); the behaviour is that of bash 3.2. A form like `${lock[@]+\"${lock[@]}\"}` or an `if` avoids it."
          },
          {
            "file": "package-eddy/src/main.wado",
            "summary": "A review of the same head against a different `--base` is added to the existing record, which keeps the first run's base, so it is stored under the wrong base.",
            "detail": "The record path is `<reviews>/<head>.json`. If the file already exists, its `head` and `base` stay as they were and the new `Attempt` is pushed onto `reviews`. `Attempt` does not store the base it was reviewed against. So after `eddy --base X`, a later `eddy --base Y` on the same head, or the same command after `origin/main` moved and changed the merge base, reviews a different diff, but the record says it was reviewed against the first base. Anything later read from the record, such as which findings were addressed, then relates those findings to the wrong change."
          }
        ],
